This is the read-only audit report based on the current repository manifests, changelogs, roadmaps, Confluence PRD, operational manual, and Jira program structure.

Cross-repository Specialists modernization audit
Audit date: 17 July 2026
Repositories: xtrm-dev/specialists, xtrm-dev/core, Jaggerxtrm/xtmux
Program: KAN-115 — Specialists modernization
Status: decision report; no repository, Jira, Confluence, or backlog mutations performed

1. Executive finding
The modernization program is not in a clean “not started” state. A significant portion of the interactive assignment, prompt composition, progressive-disclosure, runtime-origin, tmux identity, durable message transport, and journal contracts was implemented on 14–15 July 2026, after much of the PRD baseline was formulated.

The canonical PRD remains useful for the target architecture and sequencing, but its implementation baseline and Appendix I backlog reconciliation are already stale. The PRD itself requires existing work to be classified as KEEP, REFINE, MERGE, RESEQUENCE, REPLACE, or ADD; several entries now need stronger actions: COMPLETED, SUPERSEDED, or NOT_PLANNED.

The correct first action is therefore not to dispatch KAN-116–KAN-124 as currently written. It is to perform a short reconciliation wave that closes completed legacy work, reduces partially completed epics to their residual scope, separates unrelated implementation tracks, and updates the PRD and operational manual to the current repository state.

2. Evidence limitations
The Confluence page titled open-issues.json currently contains no body content. Both its searchable text and fetched Markdown/ADF body are empty. Consequently, I could not perform an issue-by-issue transformation of the actual JSON export.

GitHub’s PR-list and PR-detail connector endpoints were intermittently returning upstream 502 errors. The repository file endpoint remained available, so this audit used the current package manifests, generated changelogs, repository roadmaps, explicit PR references embedded in those changelogs, and current implementation guidance. This is sufficient for freshness and backlog disposition, but it is not yet a review-discussion audit of every individual PR comment and diff.

3. Current repository baseline
Repository	Current manifest	PRD-era baseline	Audit result
specialists	3.20.0	3.18.2	PRD baseline is materially stale
core	0.10.6	0.10.2	Interactive launcher contract has changed
xtmux	0.1.0	SQLite V2 baseline	Most previously identified viewer/integration gaps have landed
The Specialists package is now 3.20.0, not 3.18.2. Core is now 0.10.6, not 0.10.2. xtmux has a formal package and CLI contract at 0.1.0, with its SQLite-backed observability and coordination implementation shipped in the repository.

3.1 Specialists
The scope represented by the known unitAI-6639v children is effectively complete.

sp render-task now exposes the canonical task-side renderer. The global skill paths were repaired and missing declared skills now hard-fail. Literal variables inside bead content are no longer re-expanded. using-specialists was split from a 1,416-line eager payload into a 256-line router with on-demand references. Role-envelope parity and progressive-disclosure behaviour were explicitly evaluated. The changelog records closure of unitAI-6639v.1, .4, .2.1, .2.2, .3, and .5.

Version 3.20.0 also implemented the runtime-origin bridge: direct pane-to-job binding, propagated origin for background launches, parent-job lineage, root-runtime-origin inheritance, forensic spawned_by links, high-cardinality label guards, sp ps exposure, and evidence-only lineage reconstruction.

The residual runtime-origin work is narrow. The Specialists changelog deferred one live integration test until xtmux context --current --json was available. That xtmux contract has now landed, so unitAI-z8uli should be reduced to a real-host cross-repository integration smoke, redaction verification, and closure—not treated as a new implementation epic.

The root ROADMAP.md is no longer safe as an active planning source. It still proposes a memory-curator specialist, Dolt-backed run summaries replacing flat directories, eager SessionStart injection, automatic dependency-output injection, and architecture based primarily on status.json. The current PRD instead requires pull-based memory and the operational manual explicitly says not to create a memory-curator specialist.
Disposition: archive or fully reconcile the Specialists roadmap before using any of its issue IDs for new work.

3.2 Core
Core has already implemented most of the old xtrm-k2ufi transport scope.

The launcher now obtains the rendered bead task and explicit skills from Specialists. More importantly, the implementation deliberately replaced the temporary file-transport design: Specialists owns turn-one composition; the launcher sends the identity system prompt and user assignment directly; .xtrm/role-*-prompt.md, .xtrm/role-*-task.md, @agent_prompt_file, and XTMUX_AGENT_PROMPT_FILE were removed. Pi starts with --no-skills, and requested skills are forced through the runtime-specific turn-one prefix. Preflight validation happens before worktree creation.

Core also removed roughly 35 KB of eager project-intelligence context from every Pi provider request. Architecture, rules, documentation and skill bodies are now represented by pointers or loaded on demand; only the small synthesized memory and using-xtrm operating guidance remain eager.

The launcher subsequently added literal-prefix preservation, trusted tmux transport hardening, declared-skill fallback handling, a bounded readiness wait, and a bounded wrapper-payload wait.

This supersedes the PRD’s proposed “secure prompt-file pointer delivery” as the primary contract. The current no-file turn-one composition is cleaner and has already received HOME-leak, byte-ceiling, readiness, and parity protection. The PRD and manual should describe the implemented contract rather than reopening the previous transport design.

The Core ROADMAP.md is also unsuitable as a program backlog. It contains extensive legacy skill plans, old version assumptions, old product boundaries, and long-range speculative features unrelated to the Specialists modernization program.

Disposition: close or supersede the old transport epic, retain only a narrowly defined preheated-session/reuse capability if that remains desired, and remove the Core roadmap from KAN-115 authority.

3.3 xtmux
The July 14 xtmux-gaps.md document identified topology JSON, a journal cursor, agent-instance identity, pane-to-job binding, readiness, handoff delivery, an SSH/stdio bridge, and activity spans as gaps.

All but the final telemetry-enhancement class have now substantially landed:

