No historical issue should be silently deleted. Completed or superseded issues should retain their original identity, implementation evidence and replacement reference.

5. Jira audit
KAN-115 currently groups the program into KAN-116 through KAN-124, with KAN-125 as a supporting TypeScript 7 track. The conceptual coverage is mostly correct, but the issues are too broad for sprint execution and several now mix completed and unimplemented work.

KAN-116 — Program baseline and Beads reuse
Keep, but rewrite as the immediate reconciliation gate.

It should no longer say the implementation program is entirely unstarted. Its acceptance criteria should become:

Reconcile the baseline against Specialists 3.20.0, Core 0.10.6, and current xtmux.

Close or supersede completed legacy Beads.

Complete the Beads capability/reuse matrix.

Regenerate the canonical implementation graph from only residual work.

Update the PRD, operational manual and repository roadmaps.

The gate should block new modernization dispatch, not invalidate the already-landed work retroactively.

KAN-117 — Telemetry and Eval Core
Split into two epics or independently sprintable workstreams.

Telemetry integrity must precede authoritative evaluation. One epic should own tool-call deduplication, thinking/activity spans, durations, candidate fingerprints, historical completeness and live Prometheus projection. The second should own eval storage, grader interfaces, backfill, CLI, paired experiments and promotion evidence.

Combining both creates a very large collision surface around SQLite migrations, event schemas and evaluation tables.

KAN-118 — Interactive assignment and recovery
Replace the current issue.

The interactive assignment half is largely implemented. The remaining work is predominantly logical sessions, safe Pi resume, reconciliation, input-concurrency policy and crash-injection evaluation.

Recommended replacement:

Interactive assignment residual validation: preheated-session reuse, real Pi/Claude parity smoke, failure/no-leak regression and contract documentation.

Logical sessions and safe recovery: the full R01–R08 scope.

Recovery should remain highest priority. It must not be hidden inside an epic whose first half is already complete.

KAN-119 and KAN-124 — Chain spine and deterministic context
Keep as separate delivery stages, with an explicit dependency.

KAN-119 should own durable resolved chain shape, semantic edges, Beads-backed readiness and workspace/dispatch resolution. KAN-124 should then own the deterministic per-step context and enriched handoff contract.

They should not independently own the same planning-skill or output-contract work.

KAN-120 — Coordinator alignment
Keep, later wave.

This remains a substantial unimplemented track: entry gate, borderline judgment, hygiene coordination, close-time release judgment, incident handling, autonomy journal and recovery semantics.

It must depend on telemetry/eval, chain state, logical sessions and deterministic context. It should remain shadow-only until evaluated.

KAN-121 — Runtime contracts and prompt modernization
Keep but reduce scope.

Remove progressive-disclosure and role-envelope work that has already landed. Retain:

one generated output contract;

unified verdict vocabulary;

role-aware policy injection;

GitNexus policy consolidation;

writer-only close/commit semantics;

A/B prompt evaluation and promotion.

KAN-122 — Three-lens memory
Keep with explicit exclusions.

The target remains herd, workgroup and identity lenses with provenance, pull-based retrieval, close-time distillation candidates and measurable retention policy. No dedicated memory-curator specialist should be created.

KAN-123 — Console and policy promotion
Split policy hooks from Console.

The policy kernel and hook architecture can begin in shadow mode after the Beads/reconciliation gate. Console materialization belongs later, after upstream event and evaluation schemas stabilize.

Combining them unnecessarily blocks policy experimentation on frontend readiness.

KAN-125 — TypeScript 7 migration
Move outside the critical-path hierarchy.

The TS7 plan is a supporting reliability and developer-tooling track and explicitly does not authorize changes to runtime ownership, coordinator authority, Beads semantics, Console ownership, Substrate or Channels.

It should be linked to KAN-115, not counted as a child whose completion determines whether the modernization program is done.

6. Recommended Jira hierarchy
KAN-115 should function as a program or initiative rather than one execution epic containing oversized tasks. When the Jira hierarchy supports an Initiative or equivalent, KAN-115 should be promoted to that level. Otherwise it should remain a program tracker linked to multiple independent epics.

Sequence	Proposed epic	Primary ownership
0	Program reconciliation and Beads capability gate	Specialists/documentation
1	Telemetry integrity	Specialists
2	Eval Core and experiment evidence	Specialists
3	Logical sessions and safe recovery	Specialists + Core
4	Durable chain state and deterministic context	Specialists
5	Coordinator semantics and shadow policy kernel	Specialists + Core/xtmux integration
6	Output contracts, prompt evaluation and three-lens memory	Specialists
7	Console materialization and gradual enforcement	Console + Specialists
Separate	TypeScript 7 compiler migration	Cross-repository tooling
Each epic should then contain small repository-owned tasks. Cross-repository acceptance tests should be explicit integration tasks rather than implementation details hidden in one repository’s epic.

7. Proposed sprint waves
Wave 0 — Reconciliation and closure. Update baseline versions, close landed legacy issues, resolve the empty open-issues.json, execute the Beads capability audit, update PRD/manual/roadmaps, and run the final real-host runtime-origin smoke.

Wave 1 — Evidence substrate. Complete telemetry integrity first, then establish Eval Core in observe-only mode. Freeze a reproducible baseline before any major prompt or coordinator authority change.

Wave 2 — Recovery and chain authority. Implement logical sessions, safe resume and state reconciliation. Then implement the durable chain spine and Beads-backed readiness.

Wave 3 — Context and coordination. Complete deterministic chain context and handoff semantics. Introduce coordinator roles and policy hooks only in shadow mode.

Wave 4 — Behaviour modernization. Unify output contracts and verdicts, run role-by-role prompt experiments, and introduce the pull-based three-lens memory bridge.

Wave 5 — Productization. Materialize stable contracts in Console, establish promotion dashboards, and move policies through off → shadow → warn → enforce.

8. Requirements that remain binding
The audit preserves the architectural decisions already established:

Specialists remains authoritative for jobs, chains, evidence, evaluation and logical sessions. xtmux remains authoritative for interactive lifecycle, topology, messages, receipts, monitors and handoff delivery. Core remains responsible for launcher workflow and managed skills. Beads remains the durable work contract and memory substrate. Console remains a materialized operational read model.

There should be no monorepo migration now. Substrate and Channels remain future targets and must not enter the current critical path. There should be no automatic Pi resume before reconciliation, no automatic semantic-memory writes before evaluation, no coordinator release or merge authority before calibration, and no prompt-wide refactor before telemetry and evaluation baselines exist.

9. open-issues.json transformation contract
Once the actual local export is available, its original issue fields and IDs should be preserved. Audit decisions should be additive rather than destructive, for example:

{
  "_audit": {
    "decision": "keep | close_completed | close_not_planned | merge | replace | resequence",
    "reason": "human-readable rationale",
    "evidence": [
      {
        "repository": "xtrm-dev/specialists",
        "reference": "v3.20.0 / PR #185"
      }
    ],
    "superseded_by": ["new-or-existing-id"],
    "target_program": "KAN-115",
    "target_epic": "logical-sessions-recovery",
    "wave": 2,
    "requires_human_approval": true
  }
}
This permits the local dev host to reimport the file without losing issue history or confusing a completed issue with an issue that never had value.

10. Decision
The current KAN-115 decomposition should not be dispatched unchanged.

The approved mutation wave should first:

update the PRD and operational manual baseline;

reconcile or archive the three repository roadmaps/gap documents;

close completed unitAI-6639v, xtmux V2 and landed j46 work;

reduce unitAI-z8uli to its final integration gate;

replace KAN-118 with residual assignment validation plus a dedicated recovery epic;

split telemetry from Eval Core and policy hooks from Console;

move KAN-125 outside the critical-path completion hierarchy;

regenerate the real open-issues.json audit once its content is available.

No Jira, Confluence, GitHub, or JSON changes have been made.

The next controlled step is approval of the Wave 0 mutation scope. No systems were changed.


